{
  "title": "About Similarity Plugin",
  "story": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "aec1dfcd5f2bc42b",
      "text": "The Similarity plugin adds search to a Federated Wiki farm \u2014 semantic similarity over prebuilt vector indexes, ranked search reports, galactic keyword search over each site's own MiniSearch index, and semantic search across the wider federation. One item type \u2014 **similarity** \u2014 with a line-oriented DSL, plus a server component that serves every search same-origin. Works on any host: embedding runs in-process (native ONNX where available, WASM everywhere else)."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "28edff984cc5334d",
      "text": "## The DSL\n\nThe first ALL-CAPS keyword sets the mode; other lines are domain specs (globs, explicit domains, or the scope keywords PUBLIC / LOCAL / PRIVATE / GALAXY):\n\n- (no keyword) \u2014 interactive semantic search form\n- `SIMILAR: high|medium|low` \u2014 ambient: pages similar to this page\n- `REPORT` \u2014 server-side ranked, stub-filtered, fork-bundled report\n- `KEYWORD` \u2014 galactic MiniSearch over live site-index.json files\n- `SITES` \u2014 which site should this page go on? Per-domain aggregation of the page-vector scan: 0.7 \u00d7 mean of the top 5 page similarities + 0.3 \u00d7 site-centroid similarity. Forked pages count for every site that carries them\n- `LIST` \u2014 table of indexed domains\n- `AUTHOR` \u2014 search results as a draggable ghost page\n- `BUILD` / `FORCE` \u2014 proxy to the farm indexer, where configured\n- `GHOST url` / `BUTTON label` \u2014 open any page-json as a ghost page\n- `ROSTER site/slug` \u2014 add a roster page's sites to the scope (off-farm sites searched via the galaxy cache)\n- `FARM domain` \u2014 ask a peer farm to continue the search. The peer answers from exactly the sites whose own [[Federated Farm Search]] page grants your origin (`FROM` lines; `FROM *` needs the peer admin's `WIKI_PEER_FEDERATION=open`)\n- `GALAXY` \u2014 search the off-farm federation sites indexed into this host's galaxy tree. A scope, not a mode: `GALAXY` alone answers only from the federation (the farm's own pages are never swept in, and `*` never includes galaxy sites); `*` and `GALAXY` together search farm and federation as one\n- `SUBJECT` \u2014 act on the previous page in the lineup, the host a tool page was opened beside\n- `THRESHOLD: 0.72` \u00b7 `LIMIT: 8` \u00b7 `LIVE` \u2014 tuning"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "251793ca0dae4810",
      "text": "## Try It\n\nSemantic search across the farm:"
    },
    {
      "type": "similarity",
      "id": "86aeab8be16dd8e1",
      "text": "*"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b6a9be0145bae9bd",
      "text": "Pages similar to this one:"
    },
    {
      "type": "similarity",
      "id": "cf558a16395d7d8c",
      "text": "SIMILAR: medium\nLIMIT: 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "1aceb7035c7537b2",
      "text": "A ranked report (opens as a ghost page):"
    },
    {
      "type": "similarity",
      "id": "59bfe1e3968743a1",
      "text": "REPORT\nLIMIT: 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "31b81a31f675c4e1",
      "text": "Keyword search over the sites' own indexes:"
    },
    {
      "type": "similarity",
      "id": "0c3fb9298ba8fd23",
      "text": "KEYWORD\nLIMIT: 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "4a766c3e3a46c37c",
      "text": "Which site should a page go on? Type a title and first paragraph:"
    },
    {
      "type": "similarity",
      "id": "54e625a9bb8a57cd",
      "text": "SITES\nLIMIT: 5"
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "d9a96dfebb9d1692",
      "text": "## Server Routes\n\nAll same-origin, registered by the plugin's server component:\n\n- `GET /system/indexed-domains.json?pattern=`\n- `GET /system/semantic-vectors.json?domain=`\n- `GET /system/embed.json?text=`\n- `POST /system/search-report.json` \u2014 accepts an optional `author`: a display name or account username, resolved against this farm's own site ownership (each site's `owner.json`) and intersected with the requested scope. An ambiguous name answers 409 with the competing usernames rather than guessing\n- `POST /system/site-report.json` \u2014 which site should this page go on? Per-domain aggregation of the page-vector scan: 0.7 \u00d7 mean of the top 5 page similarities + 0.3 \u00d7 site-centroid similarity; forked pages count for every site that carries them\n- `GET /system/farm-search.json?q=&pattern=&limit=`\n- `GET /system/build-index.json?domains=` (proxied to the farm indexer)\n- `GET /system/galaxy-search.json?q=&sites=` \u2014 keyword search over off-farm federation sites\n- `GET /system/galaxy-registry.json` \u2014 the galaxy site registry: every federation site known to this host, with provenance, liveness, page counts and scraped site owner (read-only; written by the galaxy indexer host)\n- `GET /system/peer-hello.json` \u2014 capability probe: version, model, federation status (404 = plugin absent)\n- `GET /system/similarity-health.json` \u2014 embedder supervisor state: child-process / semindex / url, breaker status, recent crashes\n- `POST /system/peer-search.json` \u2014 answer another farm's search. An optional `author` travels as the plain name asked for, never a resolved username: the answering farm resolves it against its OWN records and reports `applied`, `ignored` (no records kept) or `ambiguous`. Two keys: the admin's `WIKI_PEER_FEDERATION` ceiling (off / grants / open) and each site's Federated Farm Search grants page; scope = union of granting sites; hop limit, replay dedup, IP-keyed + global rate limits; restricted sites always excluded\n\nIndexes are `status/semantic-vectors.json` per domain \u2014 built by the farm indexer, distributed by sync, read from farm disk. Keyword search reads each site's per-edit `status/site-index.json`; no separate index is built. Query embedding is BAAI bge-small-en-v1.5, 384-dim, CLS-pooled, unit-normalised \u2014 byte-compatible with the index builder.\n\nSince 0.9.0 all query embedding runs in a supervised child process, never in the wiki server itself: a crash in the ONNX runtime fails that one search, and repeated crashes trip a circuit breaker that answers 503 while cooling down. The worst case for a wiki running this plugin is \"semantic search temporarily unavailable\" \u2014 never a dead wiki.\n\nSince 0.11.0 a third index tier sits beside the farm: the **galaxy** \u2014 a farm-shaped tree of off-farm federation sites, built by a separate indexer and read through a byte-capped vector cache (`WIKI_GALAXY_VECTOR_CACHE_MB`, default 512). Galaxy entries carry their enrichment precomputed, because off-farm pages have no local page JSON to read; the stub filter, fork bundling and scoring then run unchanged, so federation results rank directly against farm results.\n\nSince 0.12.0 the plugin also offers a **Semantic Search** page through the wiki's \u2630 menu, declared by a `pages` entry in `factory.json` and shipped in the package.\n\nSince 0.13.0 restricted sites are hidden from **every** route unless the caller is trusted, not only from peer answers. Restricted = the local farm's `wikiDomains` entries marked `restricted`, the globs in `WIKI_RESTRICTED_DOMAINS` (e.g. `*.private.fish`), and extra farms' config files. Trusted = an owner or admin session, a tool on the same host (loopback, no `X-Forwarded-For`), or a client the reverse proxy vouches for from `WIKI_TRUSTED_NETS` (default: the Tailscale ranges and loopback; the header is believed only when the direct peer is loopback). Cross-origin browser requests are never trusted. Untrusted callers get the public view: `semantic-vectors.json` answers 403 for a restricted domain, and the scans simply do not include it. `similarity-health.json` reports the policy and `callerTrusted`."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b4e1c0d2a7f39e56",
      "text": "0.14.0 decides where the query embedder lives, and says so out loud. `WIKI_EMBED_URL` is now validated: a value that is not an http(s) URL \u2014 the public farm had the literal string `disabled` \u2014 is ignored with a warning instead of being POSTed to, which is what whoever typed it meant. Where the environment is not yours to set, the address can come from `{farmRoot}/similarity.json` (`{\"embedUrl\": \"\u2026\"}`) instead, read mtime-cached so a retarget needs no restart. `/system/similarity-health.json` now reports which `source` won and whether a `localEmbedderAvailable` \u2014 the local path is never exercised while a proxy is set, so that question was otherwise unanswerable from outside. Both HTTP helpers also learned `https`: before this they were http-only on port 80, so an https proxy could never have worked."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "c72e1a4f08b5d693",
      "text": "0.15.0 makes a dead embedder degrade honestly. The embedder and the keyword index share nothing, so one can be down while the other is fine \u2014 but a report that could not embed its query used to leave a bare status code and no results. It now says semantic search is unavailable, gives the reason, and answers the same query by keyword instead. The result page is titled \u201cKeyword Search\u201d by the server, so the reader can see which tier answered. 0.15.1 makes that fallback actually fire: an unreachable remote embedder arrived as a bare 500, indistinguishable from a bad query, so the client had nothing to trigger on. A proxy that cannot be reached is now the same outage as a local embedder that will not start, and answers 503."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "e91b3c7d25a4f608",
      "text": "0.16.0 ships a **Search Tool** page and the two DSL words it needed. `STATUS` renders the state of the index behind the answers \u2014 which model, how many domains and pages, when the vectors were newest and oldest built, and whether a query can be embedded at all. `SITE` means the domain the page is served from, so a page shipped to every site in a farm can say \"search here\" without naming anywhere; leaving the scope empty means the whole farm in the report modes, which is not the same thing. `indexed-domains.json` gained a `built` date per domain to feed the status panel. 0.16.1 makes STATUS tell the truth about a delegated embedder: it now answers \"is search up?\" by embedding a word, not by reading a supervisor field that a proxy never sets \u2014 a farm pointed at a dead address was reporting itself healthy. 0.16.2 stops a scoped search claiming \"0 pages\": an explicit domain is taken at its word rather than looked up, so its page count is unknown, not zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "a5f7d20c9e13b846",
      "text": "0.17.0 takes over the **search door**. The `search` link at the foot of every page, and the search box beside it, both built their result page in JavaScript \u2014 a fixed set of items no owner could edit. They now open the Search Tool page instead, positioned right after the page you came from so `SUBJECT` reads it: clicking search on a page asks for pages like *that* page, with nothing typed. A query typed into the box is carried into the farm-wide search and run. If the Search Tool page is not served \u2014 an older plugin, or a server with no plugin routes \u2014 nothing is intercepted and the original behaviour stands. This is also why the plugin now declares an `editor` (which hands straight back to the standard text editor): plugin bundles load lazily by item type, and a plugin declaring an editor is the one thing wiki-client fetches on every page."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "21ecf7448823670c",
      "text": "0.18.0 makes the federation cheap to search, and does it without touching a single index file. A federation query took 46 seconds on the Pi5 \u2014 not to score 100,000 page vectors, which takes tens of milliseconds, but to parse 872 MB of JSON on every query, because the vector cache was capped in *file* bytes and could never hold a federation scope. Each site's vectors now become one Float32 matrix (1.5 KB a page instead of 8.6) held in a cache capped in heap bytes that means what it says; the first parse is written to a plugin-owned disk cache under `~/.cache/wiki-similarity/store` so a restart reads binary in milliseconds; and the store warms itself at startup, off the request path, so the first query is never the cold one and a page save never waits behind it. Every existing consumer of `page.vector` keeps working \u2014 the vectors are views into the matrix. `WIKI_GALAXY_VECTORS=off` switches the galaxy tree off for a process that should not hold a second copy. `similarity-health.json` reports the `store` \u2014 sites and pages resident, bytes against the cap, files parsed versus restored, and the warm-up state \u2014 and `STATUS` shows the same row. Directory listings are memoised for thirty seconds so a thousand stats on a USB drive are not repeated per query. The design behind this and the phases after it is [https://search.fedwiki.club/view/incremental-federated-search Incremental Federated Search]."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b6fe791b56728d2c",
      "text": "0.19.0 searches the federation a batch at a time, nearest sites first \u2014 the [https://search.fedwiki.club/view/incremental-federated-search Incremental Federated Search] design. Underneath is the **Site Index**: one vector per site for every site the galaxy registry knows (built nightly by the galaxy writer into `sites.json`, served at `galaxy-sites.json`, fetched from a peer by farms without a galaxy tree), so a query ranks thousands of sites before it reads a single page vector \u2014 the resource-selection step of federated search. `site-rank.json` returns that order: the sites you are near first (an item's `ROSTER` lines, the lineup's neighbourhood, the registry's followed tier, the reader's own [[Search Algorithm]]), then everything else by centroid similarity, ties to the freshest index. A `REPORT` whose scope reaches `GALAXY` \u2014 or any scope with `BATCH n` \u2014 then searches those sites fifty at a time through `search-report.json {flat: true}`, merging in place and stopping when three consecutive batches leave the top results unchanged, with *Search the remaining sites* behind a button and *Open as page* for the merged result. Sites that rank highly while carrying no page vectors are listed, and logged, so the galaxy scout promotes them: what people search for decides what is embedded next. The cascade for farms with no galaxy of their own: `similarity.json` names `peers` in preference order, the Site Index comes from the first, and batch domains a farm holds no vectors for are asked of that peer and merged back marked by source. **Search Algorithm** is a second plugin-served page \u2014 rosters of sites liked and visited, and an `ALGORITHM` item whose `WEIGHT`, `ALWAYS`, `NEVER` and `BATCH` lines the ranker reads \u2014 that a fork makes yours; the browser learns which sites you are near, open and click (decaying weekly, never leaving the browser), and **Remember** writes the top of that into the page's roster through an ordinary save. Off-farm pages built from items the prose count never sees are no longer dropped as stubs when they carry four or more items \u2014 27% of galaxy pages had under 120 characters of prose, most of them none \u2014 and `domains` may be a string as well as an array."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "572d1df31653a684",
      "text": "0.19.1 makes an explicit-domain batch cheap. A scope of fifty named domains was matched against every directory of a 1,400-site tree with the glob matcher, which builds a matrix per comparison \u2014 a second and a half per batch on the Pi5. A pattern with no wildcard is now compared by equality."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "d3a90f5b71c26e48",
      "text": "0.20.0 answers **SITES over GALAXY** by the stored site vector first. Asking which site a page belongs on used to read every page vector of every federation site \u2014 104,000 of them to rank a few hundred things. The Site Index has one vector per site, so the field is narrowed to the 250 closest by centroid before any page is read, and only those are read in full for the evidence a placement answer shows. Measured on the Pi5: 971 sites considered, 250 read, 104,425 pages down to 72,910, and 2.7 s down to **0.49 s**, with the top five identical to the full scan.\n\nThe ranking here is centroid **only**. `rankSites` normally carries the reader's preferences \u2014 followed tier, rosters, learned scores \u2014 which is right for choosing what to search first for a person and wrong for placement: with them on, a followed-tier bonus pushed the second-best site out of the field and pulled in one ranked #108. Farm scope is left scanning everything: it is ours, it is bounded, and a diluted centroid should not hide a site you own."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "df7785a66e945d29",
      "text": "0.20.1 reads the **Semantic Site Graveyard**. [https://dead.fedwiki.club/view/welcome-visitors dead.fedwiki.club] is a wiki Marvin writes nightly from the galaxy registry's probe record: one page per site that is not solid, its first sentence the verdict \u2014 dead, moved, lapsed, unreliable or flaky, and where a moved site went. That makes the wiki's own `sitemap.json` a feed, and the plugin fetches it daily (conditional GET, `similarity.json` `verdicts` to point elsewhere or switch it off, `WIKI_VERDICTS` likewise). `site-rank` then drops dead and lapsed sites from every batch, searches a moved site's destination in its place, and sinks unreliable and flaky sites by `WEIGHT reliable` (default 0.3); the Search Algorithm page gains that weight and `TRUST solid|flaky|any`. `similarity-health.json` reports the feed and how many verdicts it holds. The plan is the [https://plan.ide.earth/view/dead-sites-plan Dead Sites Plan]."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "29afd97b8246eb6e",
      "text": "0.20.2 follows a moved site. A result whose site the graveyard calls dead, lapsed or moved is rewritten to where the site went when that site holds the same page, and labelled *site dead, probably moved to \u2026* otherwise \u2014 on both the page and the flat report \u2014 so a reader who searches with `TRUST any`, or a stale peer's answer, still lands on a living page. Batched results show *moved here from \u2026*."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "21eb5354d2adb29e",
      "text": "**Version:** 0.20.2\n\n# Links\n\n- [https://github.com/Hitchhikers-Guide-to-the-Galaxy/wiki-plugin-similarity GitHub repository]\n- [https://www.npmjs.com/package/wiki-plugin-similarity npm package]\n- [https://plugin.fedwiki.club/similarity-plugin.html Plugin documentation]\n- [https://search.fedwiki.club/ Search hub] \u2014 architecture, indexing, freshness."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "e2cde2572a99224e",
      "text": "**0.21.0** \u2014 the cascade beyond the first peer (Mini Indexer Plan, Phase 4). A flat report's missing domains walk the farm's peers **in order**, each asked only for what is still missing; a peer's answer says which domains it `held`, so the walk moves on with the rest, two hops at most and never back to a host already in the chain. The Cafe names only the Pi5; the Pi5 names the mini after itself; a page only the mini indexed is answered from any Cafe site, marked `via` the host that had it. The result page names who answered \u2014 this farm and each peer, by sites \u2014 when each result's site was last indexed and which indexer placed it, and how old the Site Index is."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "6c51f2131b0e7623",
      "text": "**0.22.0** \u2014 staleness (Dead Sites Plan, Phase 6). The graveyard's verdicts now include *stale* (a year without an edit, or a third of a site's references dead) and *abandoned* (three years), read from the same sitemap. `site-rank` sinks them by `WEIGHT stale` (default 0.2, abandoned twice as far as stale); a [[Search Algorithm]] with `WEIGHT stale 0` searches old sites on equal terms, and below 0 prefers them. Neither class is gone: the sites are searched, only later."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "07eec3cdb7b07eb2",
      "text": "**0.22.1** \u2014 `STATUS` reports the graveyard's verdicts as this farm last read them: how many sites, by class, from which feed and when. One line that shows a farm reading dead.fedwiki.club's sitemap and acting on it."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "4db2c2944bf88723",
      "text": "**0.23.0** \u2014 keep your place (Personal Search Plan, Phase 1). The *like the page you came from* item asks the server for one seeded report instead of pulling every site's vectors into the browser (417 requests and 167 MB on the Cafe became one request). A batched list no longer reorders under the reader: results already shown stay where they are while batches land, new results that rank above them are announced \u2014 *n new results rank above \u2014 fold in* \u2014 and folded in on request; a result the reader opened is marked and keeps its position even then. The list is remembered per item and query, so coming back to the page shows it at once and carries on from where it stopped, without re-ranking. The default batch is a hundred sites, measured to answer in under half a second on a Pi over a tailnet."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "b64b3b1cac49f7a4",
      "text": "**0.24.0** \u2014 [[Like This Page]], in the \u2630 menu on every site: pages like the one you were reading, on this farm in one request and then across the federation, starting by itself. `AUTO` runs a report item as soon as it has a query \u2014 a `SUBJECT` title, say \u2014 with no button press. `SUBJECT` now walks back past tool pages (Selected Plugin Pages, Search Tool, this page) to the page the reader was actually on, so the menu door reads the right subject. The *like this page* item reports how long its one request took, and a batched item shows a progress bar while it runs."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "ac44fae5937e27eb",
      "text": "**0.21.1** \u2014 three fixes from the first day of the second peer. A farm's copy of a peer's Site Index is checked **hourly** by conditional GET, and always once at startup, so a restart never serves a copy the peer has since rebuilt \u2014 it was six hours, and a 304 reset the clock, so a new indexer's sites could take most of a day to reach the Cafe. A domain named explicitly in a batched item is **searched whether or not the Site Index knows it**, as one more batch, since the cascade finds the peer that holds it. And a batched item's ready line reports how far the Site Index reaches rather than how many domains this host holds, which on a farm with no galaxy tree read as a misleading zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "99d1f2910f05b38d",
      "text": "**0.21.2** \u2014 a site answered two hops away was counted twice in the *answered by* line, once under the peer that relayed it and once under the peer that held it. The relay's count now excludes what its own peers answered."
    }
  ],
